1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,236
Sworn is for Tempo Zones: only the operator sees every transaction.

2
00:00:06,286 --> 00:00:09,664
It makes that private execution checkable.

3
00:00:09,714 --> 00:00:11,950
Two separate demonstrations follow.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:18,550
An operator runs a real proof job for Tempo's integration-test fixture:

5
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:25,150
deposit and withdrawal blocks five through six, on dev chain 1337.

6
00:00:25,200 --> 00:00:26,950
Nothing is sent.

7
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:29,303
The witness stays private.

8
00:00:29,353 --> 00:00:36,950
The verifier learns the batch is valid, not balances, senders, recipients or amounts.

9
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:42,283
This fixture batch was attested on Moderato earlier.

10
00:00:42,333 --> 00:00:50,950
Re-verify it live: the real batch passes; change one field, and it reverts.

11
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:55,950
Separately, on our own Zone: a different batch and verifier.

12
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:01,450
There, the portal settled three batches only after the proof passed.

13
00:01:01,500 --> 00:01:05,950
Then our sequencer, not the proof, paid a withdrawal.

14
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:10,687
A forged batch, even signed by our own sequencer,

15
00:01:10,737 --> 00:01:15,950
was rejected: a real proof cannot authorize a different batch.

16
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:19,586
Export the live verify calls.

17
00:01:19,636 --> 00:01:23,950
Re-run the tests against deployed bytecode.

18
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:27,450
Sworn turns private execution into verifiable evidence.

19
00:01:27,500 --> 00:01:33,950
On our own Zone, a withdrawal was paid only after the proof passed.
